#  LINE ENDINGS, PINNED TO THE REPO RATHER THAN TO EACH MACHINE'S GIT CONFIG.
#
#  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Twenty files read as wholly rewritten on every handoff
#  -- LICENSE, five .frames, petportsTaskAction.lua, petports_petport.lua, the
#  handoff document -- with identical line counts and no content change. The
#  cause is not the zip and not an editor: it is that line-ending handling was
#  living in `core.autocrlf`, which is a PER-MACHINE setting. A repo whose
#  correctness depends on every clone being configured the same way will drift
#  the first time it is opened somewhere else, and this one did.
#
#  `.gitattributes` is the fix because it TRAVELS WITH THE REPO. Every clone,
#  every checkout, every tool reads the same rule, and a machine with no git
#  config at all gets it right.
#
#  LF, BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT THE HISTORY ALREADY IS. 148 of 149 tracked text
#  files are committed LF; petports_contract.lua alone is CRLF and is the
#  straggler, not the standard. Starbound does not care -- Lua, JSON and the
#  asset loader all read either -- so this is chosen for diff readability and
#  nothing else.
#
#  WHAT THIS KILLS. `todo.tooling.crlfdrift` and the whole class of edit hazard
#  under it: a str_replace writing LF into a CRLF file leaves bare-LF lines that
#  are invisible in game and are the residue of an edit that CAN do damage. With
#  one ending everywhere there is no mixed state to land in, and no per-file
#  "preserve CRLF here" rule to remember.

#  Everything git considers text: normalised to LF in the repo AND checked out
#  as LF, so the working tree and the object store agree on every platform.
*                   text=auto eol=lf

#  Binaries. Marked explicitly so git never tries to translate one -- a single
#  byte changed inside a .png is silent corruption, not a diff.
*.png               binary
*.xcf               binary

#  Windows batch files are the one real exception. cmd.exe can mis-parse a .bat
#  with LF endings -- labels and goto are the usual casualties -- so these keep
#  CRLF in the working tree regardless of the rule above.
*.bat               text eol=crlf
